From b3b94aab5f3a668695b144e3083f431c8a73ffd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: verkur46 <mohamadrezasahami1999@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:53:16 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 017/230] Name the players in prediction summaries instead of
 leaving them implicit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A stored payload is keyed by ESPN athlete id, and the history screen
rendered only the resolver's numbers: "Your pick's stat score 8 / Rival's
5" says nothing about WHO, so the breakdown explained a result nobody
could place. The sub board was worse on the match page — it showed a bare
count — and the shot predictor named the player but dropped the zone,
hiding half of what was actually picked.

The history endpoint now attaches player_names for each row, resolved in
one query for the whole page: it is up to 100 rows of ~11 ids each, so
per-row lookups would be 100 round trips for one list. A new shared
pickSummary() renders every mechanic for both screens, so the match hub
and the history list cannot disagree about what a pick says. Names pass
through i18n/names.ts for Persian like every other proper noun, and
zone labels reuse the shot.zone* keys that already existed in both
languages.

Unsynced players degrade to #id rather than a blank line or a wrong
name; the test pins that no mechanic can print a raw id in EN or FA.
